2. DATASET PREPARATION
━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

Strategy: Multi-source, quality-first curation

  Source 1: Handcrafted seed examples (21 examples)
  ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
  These are the highest-quality examples, written with:
  • Detailed word-by-word breakdowns
  • Context and scriptural sources
  • Multiple translation phrasings
  • Grammar analysis where relevant
  
  Coverage: All 6 task types across famous Sanskrit texts
  (Bhagavad Gita, Upanishads, Yoga Sutras, Hitopadesha)

  Source 2: OPUS Parallel Corpora
  ────────────────────────────────
  OPUS provides Sanskrit-English sentence pairs.
  We format these as simple translation instructions.
  Quality varies — some pairs are short and clean, others noisy.
  
  Filtering applied:
  • Minimum 10 chars in source and target
  • Deduplication
  • Up to 300 examples

  Source 3: Synthetic instruction generation
  ───────────────────────────────────────────
  Generated instruction variants for the same content:
  "Translate...", "What is the English meaning of...",
  "Convert to English...", etc.
  This improves robustness to different phrasings.

  Format: Alpaca-style instruction tuning
  ────────────────────────────────────────
  ### Instruction:
  {instruction}
  
  ### Input:
  {input}         ← optional for QA/grammar tasks
  
  ### Response:
  {output}
  
  Why Alpaca format:
  • Widely used and well-understood by models
  • Clear structural separation
  • Works with completion-only loss masking
  • Supported natively by TRL/SFTTrainer

  Key decision: Completion-only loss masking
  ──────────────────────────────────────────
  We ONLY compute loss on the ### Response: tokens, not on the
  instruction/input. This is important because:
  1. We don't want the model to "memorize" prompts
  2. We want to train the model to generate good responses
  3. Avoids gradient noise from non-informative prompt tokens

  Dataset statistics:
  • Total: ~350 examples (more with OPUS)
  • Train/Val: 90/10 split
  • Task distribution: san2eng (40%), qa (25%), explanation (20%),
                       grammar (10%), eng2san (5%)

