4. FINE-TUNING APPROACH
━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

Method: QLoRA (Quantized Low-Rank Adaptation)

Why QLoRA over full fine-tuning?
  Full fine-tuning would require ~16GB VRAM just for the model weights
  in bf16 (2B × 2 bytes = 4GB), plus optimizer states (4× = 16GB),
  plus activations. This exceeds T4 capacity.

  QLoRA: 4-bit model + FP16 LoRA adapters = ~7GB total. ✓

Configuration:
  • Quantization : NF4 (NormalFloat4), double quantization enabled
  • LoRA rank    : r=16 (good expressiveness for domain adaptation)
  • LoRA alpha   : 32 (effective learning rate scaling = 2.0)
  • Target layers: q_proj, k_proj, v_proj, o_proj (attention)
                   gate_proj, up_proj, down_proj (MLP)
  • Dropout      : 0.05 (mild regularization)
  
Why target both attention AND MLP layers?
  Standard LoRA fine-tunes only Q and V projections. For Sanskrit,
  we need deeper adaptation because:
  1. Sanskrit vocabulary patterns are novel to the base model
  2. MLP layers store factual knowledge — Sanskrit facts need updating
  3. K, O projections affect attention patterns over Devanagari tokens
  At the cost of slightly more parameters (~16M vs ~8M), we get
  better adaptation.

Optimizer: paged_adamw_32bit
  Paged AdamW offloads optimizer states to CPU when GPU memory is tight.
  This enables larger effective batch sizes without OOM errors.

Scheduler: Cosine with 3% warmup
  Cosine decay is standard for instruction tuning. Warmup prevents
  early large gradient updates from disrupting the pre-trained weights.

