8. CHALLENGES ENCOUNTERED
━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

  Challenge 1: Data Scarcity for Instruction Tuning
  ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
  Raw Sanskrit text exists (Wikipedia, scriptures) but instruction-
  response pairs do not. We had to curate/create them manually.
  
  Approach: Quality over quantity — 21 high-quality seeds + augmentation
  Remaining issue: 350 examples is very small for robust fine-tuning

  Challenge 2: Tokenization Fragmentation
  ────────────────────────────────────────
  Devanagari text tokenizes into 2-3× more tokens than equivalent English.
  A 300-token Sanskrit response may consume 600+ tokens in the model's
  context window, limiting what fits in max_seq_length=512.
  
  Approach: Increased max_length, kept examples concise
  Trade-off: Truncation may cut off end of long explanations

  Challenge 3: Evaluation Ground Truth
  ──────────────────────────────────────
  Sanskrit has multiple valid translations for the same verse.
  Standard metrics like BLEU penalize valid paraphrases.
  There is no established benchmark for Sanskrit instruction following.
  
  Approach: Keyword-based evaluation + qualitative rubric
  Ideal: Human evaluation by a Sanskrit scholar

  Challenge 4: Model Access (HuggingFace Gating)
  ───────────────────────────────────────────────
  Gemma requires accepting Google's terms of service.
  In Colab, this means setting HF_TOKEN environment variable.
  
  Approach: Instructions provided; fallback to Llama-3.2-1B if needed
  
  Challenge 5: Memory Management on T4
  ──────────────────────────────────────
  T4 has limited VRAM (16GB). Without careful optimization,
  OOM errors occur mid-training.
  
  Approach: All optimizations described in Section 5

  Challenge 6: Overfitting on Small Dataset
  ──────────────────────────────────────────
  With 5 epochs on ~300 examples, overfitting is a real risk.
  The model may memorize training examples rather than generalize.
  
  Approach: 
  - Monitor val loss, stop early if val loss increases
  - LoRA dropout (0.05) for mild regularization
  - Keep val set examples diverse from train set

